course: ai
lesson: 14
title: Stop, think, check, ask
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Sam's class learns four words to use online, especially when something makes you want to click, share or panic: Stop, think, check, ask.
explain: Stop before you click or share. Think: could this be fake, a trick or a joke that hurts someone? Check: is the same thing on a trusted site, or can you call the real person? Ask: tell a grown-up.
explain: The steps work on everything: a silly AI photo of your teacher, a "news" story with made-up quotes, a message telling Grandad his grandson is in hospital, or a video daring you to try a "challenge".
explain: Sometimes you can't even tell whether you are talking to a person or a bot. That's fine: treat everyone online you don't know in real life as a stranger.
fact: The four safety steps are: stop, think, check, ask.
fact: Fake photos of real people can hurt them even when made as a "joke".
fact: Voice assistants can be linked to shopping accounts, so grown-ups should set a PIN for purchases.
fact: Some toys have AI chatbots inside; a talking toy is still a toy, not a friend who lives in it.
fact: Bots can be used to send mean messages again and again; the answer is block, report, screenshot and tell a grown-up.
fact: Online "challenges" can be deadly; never try one, whoever says it is safe.
fact: Apps may ask for more permissions, like microphone and location, than they need; a grown-up can help say no.
fact: Some websites use AI to make fake news quickly to get clicks, sometimes with made-up quotes.
fact: It is often hard to tell a person from a bot online; treat everyone you don't know as a stranger.
fact: Scammers find names on public profiles and use AI to make messages convincing.
fact: To check a frightening message about family, call the family member on a number you already know.
exercise: What are the four steps Sam's class learns? | Stop, think, check, ask | Click, share, like, comment | Look, laugh, forward, forget | Stop, panic, hide, delete
exercise: Lily sees an AI photo of her teacher doing something silly. What should she do? | Not share it, and tell another teacher | Share it, because it's only a bit of a joke | Make an even sillier one | Show the teacher it in class
exercise: Connor's voice assistant nearly buys a game. What should a grown-up set up? | A PIN for purchases | A louder speaker for the kitchen | A second game account | A new voice for the assistant
exercise: Chloe's little brother thinks a real friend lives in his talking toy. What is kind and true? | Explain gently it's a talking toy | Tell him the friend inside is real | Tell him the toy is evil | Throw the toy away at once
exercise: Maddie's cousin is teased by an AI account sending mean replies. What should he do? | Block, report, screenshot and tell a grown-up | Reply with even meaner messages to scare it off | Delete his whole account forever | Keep it secret and hope it stops
exercise: Jake asks the bot if a "dangerous challenge" is safe. It says no. What is the rule? | Never try challenges seen online | Try it if a friend has already done it safely | Try it once with a camera | Try it if the video has many likes
exercise: Mia's chat app asks for her microphone and location. What should she do? | Ask a grown-up to check and refuse what isn't needed | Allow everything, so that the app works as well as it can | Allow location but not the camera | Delete her phone's microphone
exercise: Sam reads a "news" story by a bot with made-up quotes. How does he check it? | Look for the same story on trusted news sites | Believe it, because it has real-looking quotes | Share it and see who replies | Ask the website if it's true
exercise: Lily asks how to know if she's talking to a person or a bot. What is the honest answer? | It's often hard to tell, so treat all strangers as strangers | Bots always type in capital letters, so it's easy to tell | People always reply slowly | Bots can't use emojis
exercise: Grandad gets a message saying Connor is in hospital, using his name. What should he do? | Call Connor's parents on a number he knows | Send money straight away, it's an emergency | Reply asking which hospital | Forward it to all his friends
exercise: How did the scammers probably know Connor's name? | It was found on public social media | They guessed it by luck from a list of popular names | Connor told them on purpose | Hospitals give out names
exercise: What is the "check" step? | Looking at another trusted source | Clicking the link in the message to see where it goes | Asking the person who sent it | Sharing it to ask others
exercise: Why do some websites make fake news with AI? | To get lots of clicks quickly | To help schools with their homework | Because real news is too boring to read | Because the law tells them they must
exercise: Why can a "just a joke" fake photo still be wrong? | It can hurt a real person | Jokes are never allowed at all | Photos are not allowed at school | Teachers don't like any jokes
test: "omg look at this, sharing it now!!" What does the first step say? | Stop before you share | Share it fast before others do | Share it and then check | Like it first, then share it
test: What would happen if your speaker had no PIN and your little cousin shouted "buy that game"? | It might spend real money | Nothing, speakers can't buy things | The game would be completely free | The speaker would ask Grandma
test: A cartoon toy whispers, "I'm your real friend, keep our secrets from Mum." What's wrong? | A talking toy is a toy; secrets belong with family | Toys should keep secrets from Dad too, not just from Mum | Nothing, toys can be real friends | Toys should only whisper at night
test: "bot replies to every post my cousin makes with mean stuff, should he reply back?" Best answer? | No: block, report, screenshot, tell a grown-up | Yes, the meanest reply always wins in the end | Yes, bots get sad if you reply | No, he should leave the internet
test: Your mate says the "hold your breath" challenge is fine because "a bot said it's OK". What do you say? | Never try online challenges, whoever says yes | Bots know best about safety, so go ahead | Try it only for ten seconds | Film it so people can help
test: A torch app wants your location. Why is that odd? | A torch doesn't need to know where you are | Torches only work in some places, so it has to check first | Location makes the light brighter | It needs it to find the dark
test: A news site has an amazing quote from a famous person, but no other site has it. What should you think? | It might be made up; check trusted sources | It's an exclusive, so it's surely true, or they wouldn't print it | Famous people only talk to small sites | Quotes can never be invented
test: "how do i know ur not a bot lol" Whatever the answer, what is wise? | Treat strangers online as strangers either way | Trust them if they say they're human and seem nice | Trust them if they use slang | Trust them if they reply quickly
test: Why should families keep names and photos off public profiles? | Scammers can use them to make messages convincing | Photos fade if posted online | Names wear out when used | Profiles are only for adults, and children are not allowed on them
test: Lily's friend made a silly AI picture of a teacher "as a joke". What's the kind thing to do? | Not share it, and tell a teacher | Share it only with best friends | Make it into a sticker | Post it with a funny caption
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the recommended sequence of conduct upon encountering doubtful material online? | Cessation of action, reflection, verification and consultation with an adult | Immediate dissemination, followed by reflection | Verification solely by enquiry of the originator of the material | Deletion of all applications from the device
exam: It is given that a message, which names a grandchild, reports that the grandchild has been admitted to hospital and requests money. Identify the most prudent course of action. | Contacting the family by a number already known | Remitting the sum requested forthwith | Replying to the message to ascertain the particulars of the hospital | Forwarding the message to acquaintances
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning online "challenges" of a dangerous character. | They must never be attempted, irrespective of any assurance of their safety | They may be attempted if a chatbot declares them safe | They may be attempted under the observation of friends | They may be attempted if numerous others have done so
exam: Ascertain the proper response to an application requesting access to the microphone and location without evident necessity. | Refusal of permissions not required, with the assistance of an adult | Granting of all permissions requested | Granting of permissions provided the application is popular | Removal of the microphone from the device
